23:["$","$L29","4",{"block":{"type":"drag-drop","mode":"sort","title":"Phishing or probably real?","prompt":"Drag each message to the right group. Look for the warning signs.","buckets":[{"id":"phish","label":"Looks like phishing"},{"id":"real","label":"Probably real"}],"items":[{"id":"a","text":"“Your mailbox will be deactivated. Enter your password here to keep it,” from a lookalike domain","bucket":"phish"},{"id":"b","text":"Denise, from dcarter@parkviewpm.example: “Staff meeting moved to 2:00 PM”","bucket":"real"},{"id":"c","text":"“You won a $500 gift card! Pay a $4.99 fee to claim it”","bucket":"phish"},{"id":"d","text":"A vendor's “new bank details, please pay today” message you didn't expect","bucket":"phish"},{"id":"e","text":"City Water, from billing@citywater.example: “Your October statement is ready” with no request for a password","bucket":"real"},{"id":"f","text":"“IT here. Reply with your login so we can fix your account”","bucket":"phish"}],"explanation":"Requests for passwords, payment pressure, prizes with fees, and lookalike addresses are classic phishing. Even a message that seems real deserves a second look if it asks you to change how money is paid; call the vendor at a number you already have."}}]
